WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:11.120
So, let's define a mathematical framework
for how to deal with this. We define Skew(k)

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:19.250
as simply #G -- #C in the first k nucleotides
of the genome.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:26.570
And the skew diagram is simply a plot showing
Skew(k) against k.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:32.580
Now, let's construct the skew diagram for
the E. coli genome.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:33.570
It looks beautiful.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:40.570
Where do you think the origin of replication
in E. coli is, something we almost had no

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.300
hope to find.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:49.530
Of course, it is at the point where it [the
skew] was decreasing and suddenly starts increasing,

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:55.380
which is here. We found the replication origin
in E. coli.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:02.380
And now, lets try to find frequent words in
this origin, and hopefully, we will find DnaA

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:09.530
box in this frame.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:16.120
After we run our Frequent Words Problem, unfortunately,
it turns out there are no frequent words that

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:22.890
appear even 3 times in this replication origin,
which means that we failed.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:28.130
There are many reasons why we may fail: maybe
the origin of replication, as derived from

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:33.520
skew diagram, does not show precisely the
area we want to see.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:40.520
Or maybe, just maybe, we don't have a good
grip on what hidden messages in E. coli origin

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:43.270
look like.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:50.270
Should we give up? Let's try to proceed further
and figure out what else can be done.

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:58.190
When we were looking for frequent words, our
view of frequent words was very naïve.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:04.200
We assume that frequent words are simply k-mers.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:11.200
But maybe hidden messages that the cell uses
to initiate replication are more elusive,

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.170
more subtle.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:18.630
Let's look at this origin of replication in
Vibrio cholerae and try to see, maybe there

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:19.430
is something else.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:25.250
We already found six 9-mers in this region,
but maybe, just maybe, there is something

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:27.990
else that deserves our attention.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:33.620
[If] you look carefully there, then you will
see that in addition to these six hidden messages,

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.730
there are two more.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:42.350
There are actually two 9-mers that look almost
like canonical 9-mers, they differ just in

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.590
a single mutation.

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:51.590
And they also represent DnaA boxes because
DNA can bind not only to the perfect 9-mer

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:56.130
but to different variations of this 9-mer.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:03:03.130
So, to find this type of more elusive frequent
word, we need to solve Frequent Words with

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:10.860
Mismatches Problem when the input is a string
Text and integers k and d, and you want to

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:18.670
find all most frequent word k-mers with at
most d mismatches in Text.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:24.760
And finally, after we have done this, and
run our Frequent Words with Mismatches Problem,

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:31.470
we do find somewhat more elusive frequent
words in E. coli genome, and they turn out

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:35.860
to be real DnaA boxes in E. coli.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:42.860
Now, what I described is a very idealistic
view of how the origin of replication can

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:45.800
be found.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:52.800
In reality, some bacteria have fewer DnaA
boxes, and our Frequent Words Problem will

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:55.100
not work for finding them.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:04:02.100
The terminus of replication is often not located
directly opposite the terminus of replication,

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:08.230
and the skew diagram is often more complex
than in the case of E. coli.

